sockmap: a stream's end rides the redirect; vsock reads its whole queue

Two sockets joined by a sockmap verdict program carry a stream with no
process copying it, which is how the guest agent now joins a container's
TCP connection to its vsock stream to the host. Three things stood between
the kernel's sockmap and a correct stream:

The end of the stream. A redirected skb is sent by the target's backlog
worker, asynchronously, so the process watching the source for its FIN
and shutting the target down raced the worker and cut the tail off the
transfer (measured: a 256 MiB integrity check failed on every run). Now
the skb that ends its source's stream — the FIN for TCP, a marker for
vsock — carries a BPF_F_EOF bit through the redirect, and the backlog
shuts the target down for sending after it has sent that skb, behind
every byte before it. vsock learns of the peer's shutdown as a control
packet, not through its receive queue, so a zero-length marker is queued
behind the peer's last data when a psock is attached; a socket read any
other way never sees it. A pure FIN carries no bytes, and sending none
read as the pipe breaking (EPIPE on the target), so an empty skb is now
skipped rather than sent.

Data queued before the join. Joining hooks the socket's data-ready
callback without running it, so a request that arrived in full while the
process was still connecting the other end sat until the next packet.
TCP offers a way to run it from user space — setting SO_RCVLOWAT calls
tcp_data_ready — and vsock's set_rcvlowat now does the same.

One packet per read. virtio_transport_read_skb handed the reader one
packet per call, where tcp_read_skb drains the queue; a queue holding
more than the packet that raised data-ready (queued before the join, or
coalesced behind another) lagged by the difference forever. It now
drains the queue like TCP.

